insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_sba_consent_material_risk_001', 'SBA', '{"dateAdded":"2026-10-17","rotationIndex":172,"question":"A 52-year-old concert violinist is listed for a cervical medial branch radiofrequency denervation. The consultant does not mention a 1 in 500 risk of persistent numbness in the hand because it is very unlikely. The patient develops permanent hand numbness. Which test should be applied to decide whether the risk should have been disclosed?","choices":["Whether most pain specialists would have disclosed the risk","Whether a reasonable person in the patient''s position, or this patient, would attach significance to the risk","Whether the risk is greater than 1 in 100","Whether the patient asked specifically about risks","Whether the procedure was elective rather than urgent"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">Disclosure of risk is judged from the patient&rsquo;s perspective rather than by what the profession customarily says. A risk is material if a reasonable person in the patient&rsquo;s position would be likely to attach significance to it, or if the clinician is or should reasonably be aware that this particular patient would. For a violinist, a small risk of permanent hand numbness is likely to be material.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> The practice of a responsible body of medical opinion is the standard for treatment and diagnosis (<em>Bolam</em>), but it no longer decides what risks must be disclosed.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:8px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>B. Correct.</strong> The material-risk test from <em>Montgomery v Lanarkshire Health Board</em> (2015). It combines an objective limb (reasonable patient) and a subjective limb (this patient&rsquo;s known circumstances, such as an occupation that depends on fine hand function).</p>\n<p style=\"margin:8px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>C. Incorrect.</strong> There is no numerical cut-off. A rare risk of a severe or life-altering outcome may be material, and a more common minor risk may not be.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:8px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>D. Incorrect.</strong> Clinicians must volunteer material risks through dialogue; the duty does not depend on the patient asking.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:8px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> The duty applies to elective and non-urgent treatment alike; urgency affects what is practicable, not the standard for materiality.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Consent in UK Practice</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">Valid consent is the cornerstone of lawful treatment. In England and Wales it rests on common law, the Mental Capacity Act 2005 and GMC guidance (&ldquo;Decision making and consent&rdquo;, 2020). A competent adult may refuse treatment for any reason, rational or not, even if refusal leads to death.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Elements of Valid Consent</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Element</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Capacity</strong></td><td>The patient can understand, retain, use/weigh and communicate a decision about the specific treatment.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Voluntariness</strong></td><td>The decision is made freely, without coercion or undue pressure from clinicians, family or others.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Information</strong></td><td>The patient has been given the information needed to decide, including material risks and reasonable alternatives (including no treatment).</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">Consent can be verbal, written or implied. A signed form is evidence of consent, not the consent itself; the discussion is what matters.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Disclosure of Risk &mdash; Montgomery</div><p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><em>Montgomery v Lanarkshire Health Board</em> [2015] UKSC 11 replaced the doctor-centred (Bolam-type) approach to disclosure with a <strong>patient-centred</strong> one. The clinician must take reasonable care to ensure the patient is aware of any <strong>material risks</strong> of the treatment and of reasonable alternatives.</p><table style=\"margin:12px 0 0\"><tr><th>Point</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Test of materiality</strong></td><td>A risk is material if, in the circumstances, a <strong>reasonable person in the patient&rsquo;s position</strong> would be likely to attach significance to it, <strong>or</strong> the clinician is or should reasonably be aware that <strong>this particular patient</strong> would attach significance to it.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Not a numerical threshold</strong></td><td>Materiality is not defined by a percentage. A very small risk of a serious or life-changing outcome (e.g. permanent nerve injury in a patient who depends on manual dexterity) can be material.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dialogue</strong></td><td>Disclosure is a two-way conversation, not just handing over a leaflet or a form; the patient&rsquo;s values and circumstances must be explored.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Therapeutic exception</strong></td><td>Narrow: information may be withheld only if disclosure would be seriously detrimental to the patient&rsquo;s health. It must not be used to override the patient&rsquo;s right to decide.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Alternatives</strong></td><td>Reasonable alternatives (including conservative treatment or doing nothing) must be explained with their own risks and benefits.</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Mental Capacity Act 2005 (England and Wales)</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Principle</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>1. Presumption of capacity</strong></td><td>Every adult is presumed to have capacity unless proved otherwise (on the balance of probabilities).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2. Support</strong></td><td>A person is not treated as unable to decide until all practicable steps to help them decide have failed.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>3. Unwise decisions</strong></td><td>An unwise decision does not, by itself, show a lack of capacity.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>4. Best interests</strong></td><td>Acts or decisions for a person lacking capacity must be made in their best interests.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>5. Least restrictive</strong></td><td>The least restrictive option for the person&rsquo;s rights and freedom of action should be chosen.</td></tr></table><table style=\"margin:12px 0 0\"><tr><th>Two-stage test</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Stage 1 (diagnostic)</strong></td><td>Is there an impairment of, or disturbance in the functioning of, the mind or brain (temporary or permanent)?</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Stage 2 (functional)</strong></td><td>Because of that impairment, is the person unable to <strong>understand</strong>, <strong>retain</strong>, <strong>use or weigh</strong> the relevant information, or <strong>communicate</strong> the decision?</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">Capacity is decision-specific and time-specific; it may fluctuate. Retaining information only for a short time does not by itself mean a person lacks capacity. Any appropriately trained clinician proposing the treatment can assess capacity; a psychiatrist is not required routinely. Scotland uses the Adults with Incapacity (Scotland) Act 2000.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Children and Young People (England)</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Age / situation</th><th>Position</th></tr><tr><td><strong>16&ndash;17 years</strong></td><td>Presumed to have capacity to consent to treatment (Family Law Reform Act 1969, s.8), assessed as for adults.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Under 16 &mdash; Gillick competence</strong></td><td>A child can consent if they have sufficient understanding and intelligence to understand fully what is proposed (<em>Gillick v West Norfolk and Wisbech AHA</em> [1985]). Assessed for each decision; the more serious the decision, the greater the understanding required.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fraser guidelines</strong></td><td>Narrower criteria relating specifically to contraception and sexual health advice without parental knowledge; often wrongly used for all treatment decisions.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Refusal</strong></td><td>A refusal by a competent child under 18 can in some circumstances be overridden by someone with parental responsibility or by the court.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Parental consent</strong></td><td>If the child lacks competence, a person with parental responsibility can consent. Where clinically needed, consent from one parent is sufficient.</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">Scotland: the Age of Legal Capacity (Scotland) Act 1991 allows a person under 16 to consent if, in the opinion of a qualified medical practitioner, they understand the nature and possible consequences of the treatment.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Advance Decisions and Lasting Powers of Attorney</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Instrument</th><th>Key requirements</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Advance decision to refuse treatment (ADRT)</strong></td><td>Made by an adult with capacity. If it refuses <strong>life-sustaining treatment</strong> it must be <strong>in writing, signed and witnessed</strong>, and must state expressly that it applies <strong>even if life is at risk</strong>. Legally binding if valid and applicable; it can refuse treatment but cannot demand it.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>LPA &mdash; health and welfare</strong></td><td>The donor appoints an attorney to make health and welfare decisions once the donor lacks capacity. Must be <strong>registered with the Office of the Public Guardian</strong> before use. The attorney can refuse or consent to <strong>life-sustaining treatment only if the LPA expressly says so</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>LPA &mdash; property and financial affairs</strong></td><td>A separate instrument; it gives no authority to make treatment decisions.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>No ADRT or LPA</strong></td><td>Clinicians decide in the patient&rsquo;s best interests, consulting family/carers. If there is no one appropriate, an Independent Mental Capacity Advocate (IMCA) is instructed for serious medical treatment.</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">A court-appointed deputy can make decisions only within the scope of the court order. Scotland: Welfare Power of Attorney; Northern Ireland has its own Mental Capacity Act (NI) 2016.</p></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_mtf_mental_capacity_assessment_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-10-17","rotationIndex":173,"stem":"Regarding the assessment of mental capacity in England and Wales:","choices":["An adult is presumed to have capacity unless it is shown otherwise","A decision that appears unwise indicates that the patient lacks capacity","Capacity is assessed for each specific decision at the time it is made","Capacity assessment requires the patient to be able to use or weigh the relevant information","Inability to retain information for a short period means the patient lacks capacity"],"correct":[true,false,true,true,false],"explanation":"<p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">The Mental Capacity Act 2005 sets out a presumption of capacity and a two-stage test: an impairment or disturbance of the mind or brain, and an inability to understand, retain, use/weigh or communicate the information relevant to the decision.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>A. TRUE</strong> &mdash; The first principle of the Act is that every adult is presumed to have capacity. The burden of proving lack of capacity lies with the person asserting it, on the balance of probabilities.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>B. FALSE</strong> &mdash; An unwise decision does not on its own show a lack of capacity. Capacity concerns the decision-making process, not the outcome chosen.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>C. TRUE</strong> &mdash; Capacity is decision-specific and time-specific. A patient may be able to consent to a simple injection yet lack capacity for a complex decision, and capacity can fluctuate, for example with delirium or sedation.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>D. TRUE</strong> &mdash; The functional test asks whether the person can understand, retain, use or weigh the information and communicate their decision. Inability to use or weigh the information is a sufficient ground for lack of capacity if it results from the impairment.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>E. FALSE</strong> &mdash; The Act states that being able to retain information for only a short period does not prevent a person from being regarded as able to make the decision.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Consent in UK Practice</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">Valid consent is the cornerstone of lawful treatment. In England and Wales it rests on common law, the Mental Capacity Act 2005 and GMC guidance (&ldquo;Decision making and consent&rdquo;, 2020). A competent adult may refuse treatment for any reason, rational or not, even if refusal leads to death.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Elements of Valid Consent</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Element</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Capacity</strong></td><td>The patient can understand, retain, use/weigh and communicate a decision about the specific treatment.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Voluntariness</strong></td><td>The decision is made freely, without coercion or undue pressure from clinicians, family or others.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Information</strong></td><td>The patient has been given the information needed to decide, including material risks and reasonable alternatives (including no treatment).</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">Consent can be verbal, written or implied. A signed form is evidence of consent, not the consent itself; the discussion is what matters.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Disclosure of Risk &mdash; Montgomery</div><p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><em>Montgomery v Lanarkshire Health Board</em> [2015] UKSC 11 replaced the doctor-centred (Bolam-type) approach to disclosure with a <strong>patient-centred</strong> one. The clinician must take reasonable care to ensure the patient is aware of any <strong>material risks</strong> of the treatment and of reasonable alternatives.</p><table style=\"margin:12px 0 0\"><tr><th>Point</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Test of materiality</strong></td><td>A risk is material if, in the circumstances, a <strong>reasonable person in the patient&rsquo;s position</strong> would be likely to attach significance to it, <strong>or</strong> the clinician is or should reasonably be aware that <strong>this particular patient</strong> would attach significance to it.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Not a numerical threshold</strong></td><td>Materiality is not defined by a percentage. A very small risk of a serious or life-changing outcome (e.g. permanent nerve injury in a patient who depends on manual dexterity) can be material.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dialogue</strong></td><td>Disclosure is a two-way conversation, not just handing over a leaflet or a form; the patient&rsquo;s values and circumstances must be explored.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Therapeutic exception</strong></td><td>Narrow: information may be withheld only if disclosure would be seriously detrimental to the patient&rsquo;s health. It must not be used to override the patient&rsquo;s right to decide.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Alternatives</strong></td><td>Reasonable alternatives (including conservative treatment or doing nothing) must be explained with their own risks and benefits.</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Mental Capacity Act 2005 (England and Wales)</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Principle</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>1. Presumption of capacity</strong></td><td>Every adult is presumed to have capacity unless proved otherwise (on the balance of probabilities).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2. Support</strong></td><td>A person is not treated as unable to decide until all practicable steps to help them decide have failed.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>3. Unwise decisions</strong></td><td>An unwise decision does not, by itself, show a lack of capacity.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>4. Best interests</strong></td><td>Acts or decisions for a person lacking capacity must be made in their best interests.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>5. Least restrictive</strong></td><td>The least restrictive option for the person&rsquo;s rights and freedom of action should be chosen.</td></tr></table><table style=\"margin:12px 0 0\"><tr><th>Two-stage test</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Stage 1 (diagnostic)</strong></td><td>Is there an impairment of, or disturbance in the functioning of, the mind or brain (temporary or permanent)?</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Stage 2 (functional)</strong></td><td>Because of that impairment, is the person unable to <strong>understand</strong>, <strong>retain</strong>, <strong>use or weigh</strong> the relevant information, or <strong>communicate</strong> the decision?</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">Capacity is decision-specific and time-specific; it may fluctuate. Retaining information only for a short time does not by itself mean a person lacks capacity. Any appropriately trained clinician proposing the treatment can assess capacity; a psychiatrist is not required routinely. Scotland uses the Adults with Incapacity (Scotland) Act 2000.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Children and Young People (England)</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Age / situation</th><th>Position</th></tr><tr><td><strong>16&ndash;17 years</strong></td><td>Presumed to have capacity to consent to treatment (Family Law Reform Act 1969, s.8), assessed as for adults.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Under 16 &mdash; Gillick competence</strong></td><td>A child can consent if they have sufficient understanding and intelligence to understand fully what is proposed (<em>Gillick v West Norfolk and Wisbech AHA</em> [1985]). Assessed for each decision; the more serious the decision, the greater the understanding required.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fraser guidelines</strong></td><td>Narrower criteria relating specifically to contraception and sexual health advice without parental knowledge; often wrongly used for all treatment decisions.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Refusal</strong></td><td>A refusal by a competent child under 18 can in some circumstances be overridden by someone with parental responsibility or by the court.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Parental consent</strong></td><td>If the child lacks competence, a person with parental responsibility can consent. Where clinically needed, consent from one parent is sufficient.</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">Scotland: the Age of Legal Capacity (Scotland) Act 1991 allows a person under 16 to consent if, in the opinion of a qualified medical practitioner, they understand the nature and possible consequences of the treatment.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Advance Decisions and Lasting Powers of Attorney</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Instrument</th><th>Key requirements</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Advance decision to refuse treatment (ADRT)</strong></td><td>Made by an adult with capacity. If it refuses <strong>life-sustaining treatment</strong> it must be <strong>in writing, signed and witnessed</strong>, and must state expressly that it applies <strong>even if life is at risk</strong>. Legally binding if valid and applicable; it can refuse treatment but cannot demand it.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>LPA &mdash; health and welfare</strong></td><td>The donor appoints an attorney to make health and welfare decisions once the donor lacks capacity. Must be <strong>registered with the Office of the Public Guardian</strong> before use. The attorney can refuse or consent to <strong>life-sustaining treatment only if the LPA expressly says so</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>LPA &mdash; property and financial affairs</strong></td><td>A separate instrument; it gives no authority to make treatment decisions.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>No ADRT or LPA</strong></td><td>Clinicians decide in the patient&rsquo;s best interests, consulting family/carers. If there is no one appropriate, an Independent Mental Capacity Advocate (IMCA) is instructed for serious medical treatment.</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">A court-appointed deputy can make decisions only within the scope of the court order. Scotland: Welfare Power of Attorney; Northern Ireland has its own Mental Capacity Act (NI) 2016.</p></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_sba_consent_competent_minor_001', 'SBA', '{"dateAdded":"2026-10-17","rotationIndex":174,"question":"A 15-year-old girl with complex regional pain syndrome attends the pain clinic alone and wishes to have a lumbar sympathetic block. Her parents are unavailable. Which statement is correct?","choices":["Consent from a person with parental responsibility is always required for patients under 16","She can give valid consent if she has sufficient understanding and intelligence to understand fully what is proposed","She cannot consent to any treatment until she is 18","Her capacity to consent must be assessed by the court before the procedure","She can consent only to contraception and sexual health treatment"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">A young person under 16 is not automatically unable to consent. Competence is assessed for the specific decision and depends on their maturity and understanding, with a higher level of understanding needed as the decision becomes more serious. If she is competent, her consent is valid without parental involvement, although involving parents is encouraged.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> Parental consent is needed only if the child lacks competence. A competent child under 16 can consent in their own right.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:8px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>B. Correct.</strong> The test is whether the child has sufficient understanding and intelligence to understand fully what is proposed (<em>Gillick</em>). A complex invasive procedure requires a high level of understanding, so a careful, documented assessment is needed.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:8px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>C. Incorrect.</strong> Patients aged 16&ndash;17 are presumed to have capacity (Family Law Reform Act 1969), and younger children may be competent on assessment; 18 is not a fixed threshold for treatment consent.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:8px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>D. Incorrect.</strong> Court involvement is reserved for disputes or difficult cases, not routine assessment of competence, which any appropriately trained clinician can perform.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:8px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> The Fraser criteria are a narrower set relating to contraception advice; the Gillick principle applies to medical treatment decisions generally.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Consent in UK Practice</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">Valid consent is the cornerstone of lawful treatment. In England and Wales it rests on common law, the Mental Capacity Act 2005 and GMC guidance (&ldquo;Decision making and consent&rdquo;, 2020). A competent adult may refuse treatment for any reason, rational or not, even if refusal leads to death.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Elements of Valid Consent</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Element</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Capacity</strong></td><td>The patient can understand, retain, use/weigh and communicate a decision about the specific treatment.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Voluntariness</strong></td><td>The decision is made freely, without coercion or undue pressure from clinicians, family or others.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Information</strong></td><td>The patient has been given the information needed to decide, including material risks and reasonable alternatives (including no treatment).</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">Consent can be verbal, written or implied. A signed form is evidence of consent, not the consent itself; the discussion is what matters.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Disclosure of Risk &mdash; Montgomery</div><p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><em>Montgomery v Lanarkshire Health Board</em> [2015] UKSC 11 replaced the doctor-centred (Bolam-type) approach to disclosure with a <strong>patient-centred</strong> one. The clinician must take reasonable care to ensure the patient is aware of any <strong>material risks</strong> of the treatment and of reasonable alternatives.</p><table style=\"margin:12px 0 0\"><tr><th>Point</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Test of materiality</strong></td><td>A risk is material if, in the circumstances, a <strong>reasonable person in the patient&rsquo;s position</strong> would be likely to attach significance to it, <strong>or</strong> the clinician is or should reasonably be aware that <strong>this particular patient</strong> would attach significance to it.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Not a numerical threshold</strong></td><td>Materiality is not defined by a percentage. A very small risk of a serious or life-changing outcome (e.g. permanent nerve injury in a patient who depends on manual dexterity) can be material.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dialogue</strong></td><td>Disclosure is a two-way conversation, not just handing over a leaflet or a form; the patient&rsquo;s values and circumstances must be explored.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Therapeutic exception</strong></td><td>Narrow: information may be withheld only if disclosure would be seriously detrimental to the patient&rsquo;s health. It must not be used to override the patient&rsquo;s right to decide.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Alternatives</strong></td><td>Reasonable alternatives (including conservative treatment or doing nothing) must be explained with their own risks and benefits.</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Mental Capacity Act 2005 (England and Wales)</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Principle</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>1. Presumption of capacity</strong></td><td>Every adult is presumed to have capacity unless proved otherwise (on the balance of probabilities).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2. Support</strong></td><td>A person is not treated as unable to decide until all practicable steps to help them decide have failed.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>3. Unwise decisions</strong></td><td>An unwise decision does not, by itself, show a lack of capacity.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>4. Best interests</strong></td><td>Acts or decisions for a person lacking capacity must be made in their best interests.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>5. Least restrictive</strong></td><td>The least restrictive option for the person&rsquo;s rights and freedom of action should be chosen.</td></tr></table><table style=\"margin:12px 0 0\"><tr><th>Two-stage test</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Stage 1 (diagnostic)</strong></td><td>Is there an impairment of, or disturbance in the functioning of, the mind or brain (temporary or permanent)?</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Stage 2 (functional)</strong></td><td>Because of that impairment, is the person unable to <strong>understand</strong>, <strong>retain</strong>, <strong>use or weigh</strong> the relevant information, or <strong>communicate</strong> the decision?</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">Capacity is decision-specific and time-specific; it may fluctuate. Retaining information only for a short time does not by itself mean a person lacks capacity. Any appropriately trained clinician proposing the treatment can assess capacity; a psychiatrist is not required routinely. Scotland uses the Adults with Incapacity (Scotland) Act 2000.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Children and Young People (England)</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Age / situation</th><th>Position</th></tr><tr><td><strong>16&ndash;17 years</strong></td><td>Presumed to have capacity to consent to treatment (Family Law Reform Act 1969, s.8), assessed as for adults.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Under 16 &mdash; Gillick competence</strong></td><td>A child can consent if they have sufficient understanding and intelligence to understand fully what is proposed (<em>Gillick v West Norfolk and Wisbech AHA</em> [1985]). Assessed for each decision; the more serious the decision, the greater the understanding required.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fraser guidelines</strong></td><td>Narrower criteria relating specifically to contraception and sexual health advice without parental knowledge; often wrongly used for all treatment decisions.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Refusal</strong></td><td>A refusal by a competent child under 18 can in some circumstances be overridden by someone with parental responsibility or by the court.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Parental consent</strong></td><td>If the child lacks competence, a person with parental responsibility can consent. Where clinically needed, consent from one parent is sufficient.</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">Scotland: the Age of Legal Capacity (Scotland) Act 1991 allows a person under 16 to consent if, in the opinion of a qualified medical practitioner, they understand the nature and possible consequences of the treatment.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Advance Decisions and Lasting Powers of Attorney</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Instrument</th><th>Key requirements</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Advance decision to refuse treatment (ADRT)</strong></td><td>Made by an adult with capacity. If it refuses <strong>life-sustaining treatment</strong> it must be <strong>in writing, signed and witnessed</strong>, and must state expressly that it applies <strong>even if life is at risk</strong>. Legally binding if valid and applicable; it can refuse treatment but cannot demand it.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>LPA &mdash; health and welfare</strong></td><td>The donor appoints an attorney to make health and welfare decisions once the donor lacks capacity. Must be <strong>registered with the Office of the Public Guardian</strong> before use. The attorney can refuse or consent to <strong>life-sustaining treatment only if the LPA expressly says so</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>LPA &mdash; property and financial affairs</strong></td><td>A separate instrument; it gives no authority to make treatment decisions.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>No ADRT or LPA</strong></td><td>Clinicians decide in the patient&rsquo;s best interests, consulting family/carers. If there is no one appropriate, an Independent Mental Capacity Advocate (IMCA) is instructed for serious medical treatment.</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">A court-appointed deputy can make decisions only within the scope of the court order. Scotland: Welfare Power of Attorney; Northern Ireland has its own Mental Capacity Act (NI) 2016.</p></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_mtf_advance_decision_lpa_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-10-17","rotationIndex":175,"stem":"Regarding advance decisions and lasting powers of attorney (LPA) in England and Wales:","choices":["A health and welfare LPA must be registered with the Office of the Public Guardian before it can be used","An attorney under a health and welfare LPA can refuse life-sustaining treatment unless the LPA states otherwise","An advance decision to refuse life-sustaining treatment must be in writing, signed and witnessed","An advance decision refusing life-sustaining treatment must state that it applies even if life is at risk","A valid advance decision can be used to require a clinician to provide a specific treatment"],"correct":[true,false,true,true,false],"explanation":"<p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">Both instruments allow a person with capacity to influence future treatment decisions made when they lose capacity, but they have different formal requirements and scope under the Mental Capacity Act 2005.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>A. TRUE</strong> &mdash; An LPA has no legal authority until it is registered with the Office of the Public Guardian. A health and welfare LPA can be used only once the donor lacks capacity.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>B. FALSE</strong> &mdash; An attorney can make decisions about life-sustaining treatment only if the LPA expressly gives that authority. The default is that the attorney does not have it.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>C. TRUE</strong> &mdash; For refusal of life-sustaining treatment, the advance decision must be written (or recorded in the clinical notes at the person&rsquo;s direction), signed and witnessed. Refusals of non-life-sustaining treatment may be oral.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>D. TRUE</strong> &mdash; The document must contain an explicit statement that the refusal applies even if life is at risk. Without it, the advance decision is not valid for life-sustaining treatment.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>E. FALSE</strong> &mdash; An advance decision can only refuse treatment. Patients cannot demand a particular treatment through an advance decision or otherwise compel a clinician to provide treatment they do not consider clinically appropriate.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Consent in UK Practice</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">Valid consent is the cornerstone of lawful treatment. In England and Wales it rests on common law, the Mental Capacity Act 2005 and GMC guidance (&ldquo;Decision making and consent&rdquo;, 2020). A competent adult may refuse treatment for any reason, rational or not, even if refusal leads to death.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Elements of Valid Consent</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Element</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Capacity</strong></td><td>The patient can understand, retain, use/weigh and communicate a decision about the specific treatment.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Voluntariness</strong></td><td>The decision is made freely, without coercion or undue pressure from clinicians, family or others.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Information</strong></td><td>The patient has been given the information needed to decide, including material risks and reasonable alternatives (including no treatment).</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">Consent can be verbal, written or implied. A signed form is evidence of consent, not the consent itself; the discussion is what matters.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Disclosure of Risk &mdash; Montgomery</div><p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><em>Montgomery v Lanarkshire Health Board</em> [2015] UKSC 11 replaced the doctor-centred (Bolam-type) approach to disclosure with a <strong>patient-centred</strong> one. The clinician must take reasonable care to ensure the patient is aware of any <strong>material risks</strong> of the treatment and of reasonable alternatives.</p><table style=\"margin:12px 0 0\"><tr><th>Point</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Test of materiality</strong></td><td>A risk is material if, in the circumstances, a <strong>reasonable person in the patient&rsquo;s position</strong> would be likely to attach significance to it, <strong>or</strong> the clinician is or should reasonably be aware that <strong>this particular patient</strong> would attach significance to it.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Not a numerical threshold</strong></td><td>Materiality is not defined by a percentage. A very small risk of a serious or life-changing outcome (e.g. permanent nerve injury in a patient who depends on manual dexterity) can be material.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dialogue</strong></td><td>Disclosure is a two-way conversation, not just handing over a leaflet or a form; the patient&rsquo;s values and circumstances must be explored.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Therapeutic exception</strong></td><td>Narrow: information may be withheld only if disclosure would be seriously detrimental to the patient&rsquo;s health. It must not be used to override the patient&rsquo;s right to decide.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Alternatives</strong></td><td>Reasonable alternatives (including conservative treatment or doing nothing) must be explained with their own risks and benefits.</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Mental Capacity Act 2005 (England and Wales)</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Principle</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>1. Presumption of capacity</strong></td><td>Every adult is presumed to have capacity unless proved otherwise (on the balance of probabilities).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2. Support</strong></td><td>A person is not treated as unable to decide until all practicable steps to help them decide have failed.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>3. Unwise decisions</strong></td><td>An unwise decision does not, by itself, show a lack of capacity.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>4. Best interests</strong></td><td>Acts or decisions for a person lacking capacity must be made in their best interests.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>5. Least restrictive</strong></td><td>The least restrictive option for the person&rsquo;s rights and freedom of action should be chosen.</td></tr></table><table style=\"margin:12px 0 0\"><tr><th>Two-stage test</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Stage 1 (diagnostic)</strong></td><td>Is there an impairment of, or disturbance in the functioning of, the mind or brain (temporary or permanent)?</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Stage 2 (functional)</strong></td><td>Because of that impairment, is the person unable to <strong>understand</strong>, <strong>retain</strong>, <strong>use or weigh</strong> the relevant information, or <strong>communicate</strong> the decision?</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">Capacity is decision-specific and time-specific; it may fluctuate. Retaining information only for a short time does not by itself mean a person lacks capacity. Any appropriately trained clinician proposing the treatment can assess capacity; a psychiatrist is not required routinely. Scotland uses the Adults with Incapacity (Scotland) Act 2000.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Children and Young People (England)</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Age / situation</th><th>Position</th></tr><tr><td><strong>16&ndash;17 years</strong></td><td>Presumed to have capacity to consent to treatment (Family Law Reform Act 1969, s.8), assessed as for adults.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Under 16 &mdash; Gillick competence</strong></td><td>A child can consent if they have sufficient understanding and intelligence to understand fully what is proposed (<em>Gillick v West Norfolk and Wisbech AHA</em> [1985]). Assessed for each decision; the more serious the decision, the greater the understanding required.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fraser guidelines</strong></td><td>Narrower criteria relating specifically to contraception and sexual health advice without parental knowledge; often wrongly used for all treatment decisions.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Refusal</strong></td><td>A refusal by a competent child under 18 can in some circumstances be overridden by someone with parental responsibility or by the court.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Parental consent</strong></td><td>If the child lacks competence, a person with parental responsibility can consent. Where clinically needed, consent from one parent is sufficient.</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">Scotland: the Age of Legal Capacity (Scotland) Act 1991 allows a person under 16 to consent if, in the opinion of a qualified medical practitioner, they understand the nature and possible consequences of the treatment.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Advance Decisions and Lasting Powers of Attorney</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Instrument</th><th>Key requirements</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Advance decision to refuse treatment (ADRT)</strong></td><td>Made by an adult with capacity. If it refuses <strong>life-sustaining treatment</strong> it must be <strong>in writing, signed and witnessed</strong>, and must state expressly that it applies <strong>even if life is at risk</strong>. Legally binding if valid and applicable; it can refuse treatment but cannot demand it.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>LPA &mdash; health and welfare</strong></td><td>The donor appoints an attorney to make health and welfare decisions once the donor lacks capacity. Must be <strong>registered with the Office of the Public Guardian</strong> before use. The attorney can refuse or consent to <strong>life-sustaining treatment only if the LPA expressly says so</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>LPA &mdash; property and financial affairs</strong></td><td>A separate instrument; it gives no authority to make treatment decisions.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>No ADRT or LPA</strong></td><td>Clinicians decide in the patient&rsquo;s best interests, consulting family/carers. If there is no one appropriate, an Independent Mental Capacity Advocate (IMCA) is instructed for serious medical treatment.</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">A court-appointed deputy can make decisions only within the scope of the court order. Scotland: Welfare Power of Attorney; Northern Ireland has its own Mental Capacity Act (NI) 2016.</p></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_sba_ethics_resource_allocation_001', 'SBA', '{"dateAdded":"2026-10-17","rotationIndex":176,"question":"A pain service can fund 10 spinal cord stimulator implants this year, but 40 patients meet the criteria. The team ranks them using transparent criteria of clinical need and likely benefit, applied equally to all. Which ethical principle is this most directly applying?","choices":["Autonomy","Beneficence","Non-maleficence","Justice","Fidelity"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">Fair and transparent distribution of a scarce resource between competing patients is the core of distributive justice: like cases are treated alike and any difference in treatment is based on relevant criteria.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> Autonomy concerns respecting an individual patient&rsquo;s decision, which is not the issue in allocating a limited resource between patients.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:8px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>B. Incorrect.</strong> Beneficence is concerned with doing good for an individual patient. It informs the criterion of likely benefit, but the principle that structures the process is fairness between patients.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:8px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>C. Incorrect.</strong> Non-maleficence concerns avoiding harm. Selection of patients is not mainly about avoiding harm to a given patient.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:8px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>D. Correct.</strong> Justice covers fair allocation of limited resources, using relevant, transparent and consistently applied criteria.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:8px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> Fidelity is the duty to keep promises and maintain trust within the therapeutic relationship; it is not one of the four core principles.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Medical Ethics and Law</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">Ethical analysis in UK medicine commonly uses the four principles of Beauchamp and Childress. Legal duties on confidentiality, candour and the standard of care sit alongside these and are frequently tested in FFPMRCA medicolegal questions.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Four Principles of Biomedical Ethics</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Principle</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Pain medicine example</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Autonomy</strong></td><td>Respect for the capacitous patient&rsquo;s right to make their own decisions.</td><td>Accepting a patient&rsquo;s informed refusal of an injection.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Beneficence</strong></td><td>Act to benefit the patient.</td><td>Offering an effective, evidence-based treatment plan.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-maleficence</strong></td><td>Avoid causing harm.</td><td>Declining to escalate opioids when harms outweigh benefits.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Justice</strong></td><td>Fair distribution of benefits, risks and scarce resources; equal cases treated equally.</td><td>Allocating limited neuromodulation implants by transparent criteria of need and likely benefit.</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">Principles can conflict (e.g. autonomy vs non-maleficence); the clinician&rsquo;s task is to balance them. Double effect, paternalism and fidelity are related concepts.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Confidentiality (GMC, UK GDPR and Data Protection Act 2018)</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Position</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Direct care</strong></td><td>Information can be shared within the team providing direct care on the basis of <strong>implied consent</strong>, provided patients are aware of how their information is used.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Family and friends</strong></td><td>Adults with capacity decide who is told; relatives have no automatic right to information.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Required by law</strong></td><td>Disclosure is required by statute or court order (e.g. notifiable diseases, some regulatory requests).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Public interest</strong></td><td>Disclosure without consent may be justified to prevent or detect serious crime or serious harm; take only the information needed, tell the patient first where safe to do so, and document the reasoning.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Driving</strong></td><td>If a patient continues to drive against advice despite a condition that makes driving unsafe, the GMC advises informing the <strong>DVLA medical adviser</strong>, after warning the patient.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>After death</strong></td><td>The duty of confidentiality continues after a patient&rsquo;s death.</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Duty of Candour</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Type</th><th>Applies to</th><th>Requirement</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Statutory (England)</strong></td><td>The <strong>organisation</strong> (registered provider): Regulation 20, Health and Social Care Act 2008 (Regulated Activities) Regulations 2014</td><td>After a <strong>notifiable safety incident</strong> (unintended or unexpected incident that results in, or could result in, death or severe/moderate harm or prolonged psychological harm), tell the patient as soon as reasonably practicable, give a truthful account, apologise, offer support and follow up in writing.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Professional</strong></td><td><strong>Individual</strong> clinicians (GMC/NMC joint guidance)</td><td>Be open and honest with patients when something goes wrong or may have gone wrong: tell the patient, apologise, offer remedy or support, explain short- and long-term effects, and report the incident.</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">An apology or explanation is not an admission of liability (Compensation Act 2006, s.2). Candour arrangements differ in Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Clinical Negligence (English Law)</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Element / test</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Elements</strong></td><td>Claimant must prove <strong>duty of care</strong>, <strong>breach</strong> of that duty, <strong>causation</strong> and <strong>damage</strong>. Breach without damage is not actionable.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Standard of care &mdash; Bolam</strong></td><td>A clinician is not negligent if they acted in accordance with a practice accepted as proper by a responsible body of medical opinion (<em>Bolam v Friern Hospital Management Committee</em> [1957]). Applied to diagnosis and treatment.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Bolitho</strong></td><td>The supporting body of opinion must be <strong>capable of withstanding logical analysis</strong> (<em>Bolitho v City and Hackney HA</em> [1997]); the court may reject a practice that is not logically defensible.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Disclosure of risk</strong></td><td>Not Bolam: judged by the material-risk test in <em>Montgomery</em> (see Consent in UK Practice).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Causation</strong></td><td>&ldquo;But for&rdquo; the breach, on the <strong>balance of probabilities</strong>, would the harm have been avoided?</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hindsight</strong></td><td>The standard is judged on the knowledge and practice at the time of the event, not with hindsight.</td></tr></table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_mtf_confidentiality_disclosure_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-10-17","rotationIndex":177,"stem":"Regarding patient confidentiality in UK practice:","choices":["Relevant information can be shared with the direct care team on the basis of implied consent","An adult patient''s relatives have an automatic right to information about the patient''s condition","The duty of confidentiality ends when the patient dies","A clinician may inform the DVLA if a patient continues to drive despite being advised not to","Written consent from the patient is required before any disclosure in any circumstances"],"correct":[true,false,false,true,false],"explanation":"<p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">Confidentiality is a professional and legal duty (GMC guidance, common law, UK GDPR and the Data Protection Act 2018), but it is not absolute.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>A. TRUE</strong> &mdash; Where patients understand how their information will be used, sharing within the team providing their care is justified by implied consent, with only the information needed being shared.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>B. FALSE</strong> &mdash; Adult patients with capacity decide who is told. Relatives have no automatic entitlement; sharing needs the patient&rsquo;s consent, or a lawful basis such as best interests if the patient lacks capacity.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>C. FALSE</strong> &mdash; The duty continues after death, although there may be justification for disclosure, for example to a coroner or where it is in the public interest.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>D. TRUE</strong> &mdash; If a patient continues to drive when unfit, after being advised to stop, disclosure to the DVLA medical adviser is justified in the public interest. The patient should be told beforehand where possible.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>E. FALSE</strong> &mdash; Disclosure without consent is allowed where required by law (e.g. court order) or justified in the public interest, such as preventing serious harm, and verbal consent is acceptable for disclosures that need consent.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Medical Ethics and Law</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">Ethical analysis in UK medicine commonly uses the four principles of Beauchamp and Childress. Legal duties on confidentiality, candour and the standard of care sit alongside these and are frequently tested in FFPMRCA medicolegal questions.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Four Principles of Biomedical Ethics</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Principle</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Pain medicine example</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Autonomy</strong></td><td>Respect for the capacitous patient&rsquo;s right to make their own decisions.</td><td>Accepting a patient&rsquo;s informed refusal of an injection.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Beneficence</strong></td><td>Act to benefit the patient.</td><td>Offering an effective, evidence-based treatment plan.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-maleficence</strong></td><td>Avoid causing harm.</td><td>Declining to escalate opioids when harms outweigh benefits.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Justice</strong></td><td>Fair distribution of benefits, risks and scarce resources; equal cases treated equally.</td><td>Allocating limited neuromodulation implants by transparent criteria of need and likely benefit.</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">Principles can conflict (e.g. autonomy vs non-maleficence); the clinician&rsquo;s task is to balance them. Double effect, paternalism and fidelity are related concepts.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Confidentiality (GMC, UK GDPR and Data Protection Act 2018)</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Position</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Direct care</strong></td><td>Information can be shared within the team providing direct care on the basis of <strong>implied consent</strong>, provided patients are aware of how their information is used.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Family and friends</strong></td><td>Adults with capacity decide who is told; relatives have no automatic right to information.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Required by law</strong></td><td>Disclosure is required by statute or court order (e.g. notifiable diseases, some regulatory requests).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Public interest</strong></td><td>Disclosure without consent may be justified to prevent or detect serious crime or serious harm; take only the information needed, tell the patient first where safe to do so, and document the reasoning.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Driving</strong></td><td>If a patient continues to drive against advice despite a condition that makes driving unsafe, the GMC advises informing the <strong>DVLA medical adviser</strong>, after warning the patient.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>After death</strong></td><td>The duty of confidentiality continues after a patient&rsquo;s death.</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Duty of Candour</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Type</th><th>Applies to</th><th>Requirement</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Statutory (England)</strong></td><td>The <strong>organisation</strong> (registered provider): Regulation 20, Health and Social Care Act 2008 (Regulated Activities) Regulations 2014</td><td>After a <strong>notifiable safety incident</strong> (unintended or unexpected incident that results in, or could result in, death or severe/moderate harm or prolonged psychological harm), tell the patient as soon as reasonably practicable, give a truthful account, apologise, offer support and follow up in writing.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Professional</strong></td><td><strong>Individual</strong> clinicians (GMC/NMC joint guidance)</td><td>Be open and honest with patients when something goes wrong or may have gone wrong: tell the patient, apologise, offer remedy or support, explain short- and long-term effects, and report the incident.</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">An apology or explanation is not an admission of liability (Compensation Act 2006, s.2). Candour arrangements differ in Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Clinical Negligence (English Law)</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Element / test</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Elements</strong></td><td>Claimant must prove <strong>duty of care</strong>, <strong>breach</strong> of that duty, <strong>causation</strong> and <strong>damage</strong>. Breach without damage is not actionable.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Standard of care &mdash; Bolam</strong></td><td>A clinician is not negligent if they acted in accordance with a practice accepted as proper by a responsible body of medical opinion (<em>Bolam v Friern Hospital Management Committee</em> [1957]). Applied to diagnosis and treatment.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Bolitho</strong></td><td>The supporting body of opinion must be <strong>capable of withstanding logical analysis</strong> (<em>Bolitho v City and Hackney HA</em> [1997]); the court may reject a practice that is not logically defensible.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Disclosure of risk</strong></td><td>Not Bolam: judged by the material-risk test in <em>Montgomery</em> (see Consent in UK Practice).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Causation</strong></td><td>&ldquo;But for&rdquo; the breach, on the <strong>balance of probabilities</strong>, would the harm have been avoided?</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hindsight</strong></td><td>The standard is judged on the knowledge and practice at the time of the event, not with hindsight.</td></tr></table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
