insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_sba_duty_of_candour_wrong_site_block_001', 'SBA', '{"dateAdded":"2026-10-17","rotationIndex":178,"question":"During a nerve block, the pain physician realises the wrong side was injected. There is no obvious harm. Which action is most appropriate?","choices":["Tell the patient only if symptoms develop","Tell the patient promptly, apologise, explain what happened and the next steps, and document the conversation","Wait until a formal investigation is complete before informing the patient","Inform the patient''s GP only","Avoid apologising to prevent an admission of liability"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">Clinicians have a professional duty to be open and honest when something goes wrong, and providers in England have a statutory duty to inform the patient of a notifiable safety incident. The professional duty to be open applies whether or not harm results.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> The duty is not conditional on harm having become apparent; a patient is entitled to know that an error occurred.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:8px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>B. Correct.</strong> Prompt disclosure with an apology, a truthful account, explanation of consequences and follow-up support, together with accurate records and incident reporting, is expected by the GMC/NMC and, for notifiable incidents, by Regulation 20.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:8px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>C. Incorrect.</strong> Informing the patient as soon as reasonably practicable should not wait for the outcome of an investigation; further information can be given later.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:8px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>D. Incorrect.</strong> Telling only the GP deprives the patient of the information and does not satisfy the duty.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:8px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> An apology or statement of regret is not an admission of liability (Compensation Act 2006, s.2). Avoiding an apology is contrary to professional guidance.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Medical Ethics and Law</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">Ethical analysis in UK medicine commonly uses the four principles of Beauchamp and Childress. Legal duties on confidentiality, candour and the standard of care sit alongside these and are frequently tested in FFPMRCA medicolegal questions.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Four Principles of Biomedical Ethics</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Principle</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Pain medicine example</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Autonomy</strong></td><td>Respect for the capacitous patient&rsquo;s right to make their own decisions.</td><td>Accepting a patient&rsquo;s informed refusal of an injection.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Beneficence</strong></td><td>Act to benefit the patient.</td><td>Offering an effective, evidence-based treatment plan.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-maleficence</strong></td><td>Avoid causing harm.</td><td>Declining to escalate opioids when harms outweigh benefits.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Justice</strong></td><td>Fair distribution of benefits, risks and scarce resources; equal cases treated equally.</td><td>Allocating limited neuromodulation implants by transparent criteria of need and likely benefit.</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">Principles can conflict (e.g. autonomy vs non-maleficence); the clinician&rsquo;s task is to balance them. Double effect, paternalism and fidelity are related concepts.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Confidentiality (GMC, UK GDPR and Data Protection Act 2018)</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Position</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Direct care</strong></td><td>Information can be shared within the team providing direct care on the basis of <strong>implied consent</strong>, provided patients are aware of how their information is used.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Family and friends</strong></td><td>Adults with capacity decide who is told; relatives have no automatic right to information.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Required by law</strong></td><td>Disclosure is required by statute or court order (e.g. notifiable diseases, some regulatory requests).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Public interest</strong></td><td>Disclosure without consent may be justified to prevent or detect serious crime or serious harm; take only the information needed, tell the patient first where safe to do so, and document the reasoning.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Driving</strong></td><td>If a patient continues to drive against advice despite a condition that makes driving unsafe, the GMC advises informing the <strong>DVLA medical adviser</strong>, after warning the patient.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>After death</strong></td><td>The duty of confidentiality continues after a patient&rsquo;s death.</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Duty of Candour</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Type</th><th>Applies to</th><th>Requirement</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Statutory (England)</strong></td><td>The <strong>organisation</strong> (registered provider): Regulation 20, Health and Social Care Act 2008 (Regulated Activities) Regulations 2014</td><td>After a <strong>notifiable safety incident</strong> (unintended or unexpected incident that results in, or could result in, death or severe/moderate harm or prolonged psychological harm), tell the patient as soon as reasonably practicable, give a truthful account, apologise, offer support and follow up in writing.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Professional</strong></td><td><strong>Individual</strong> clinicians (GMC/NMC joint guidance)</td><td>Be open and honest with patients when something goes wrong or may have gone wrong: tell the patient, apologise, offer remedy or support, explain short- and long-term effects, and report the incident.</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">An apology or explanation is not an admission of liability (Compensation Act 2006, s.2). Candour arrangements differ in Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Clinical Negligence (English Law)</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Element / test</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Elements</strong></td><td>Claimant must prove <strong>duty of care</strong>, <strong>breach</strong> of that duty, <strong>causation</strong> and <strong>damage</strong>. Breach without damage is not actionable.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Standard of care &mdash; Bolam</strong></td><td>A clinician is not negligent if they acted in accordance with a practice accepted as proper by a responsible body of medical opinion (<em>Bolam v Friern Hospital Management Committee</em> [1957]). Applied to diagnosis and treatment.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Bolitho</strong></td><td>The supporting body of opinion must be <strong>capable of withstanding logical analysis</strong> (<em>Bolitho v City and Hackney HA</em> [1997]); the court may reject a practice that is not logically defensible.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Disclosure of risk</strong></td><td>Not Bolam: judged by the material-risk test in <em>Montgomery</em> (see Consent in UK Practice).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Causation</strong></td><td>&ldquo;But for&rdquo; the breach, on the <strong>balance of probabilities</strong>, would the harm have been avoided?</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hindsight</strong></td><td>The standard is judged on the knowledge and practice at the time of the event, not with hindsight.</td></tr></table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_mtf_clinical_negligence_standard_of_care_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-10-17","rotationIndex":179,"stem":"Regarding clinical negligence in English law:","choices":["A clinician is not negligent if they acted in accordance with a practice accepted as proper by a responsible body of medical opinion","A body of medical opinion must be capable of withstanding logical analysis to support a clinician''s practice","A breach of duty is actionable even if the patient suffers no damage","Causation is judged on the balance of probabilities","The standard for disclosure of treatment risks is judged by what a responsible body of doctors would disclose"],"correct":[true,true,false,true,false],"explanation":"<p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">A claim in negligence needs a duty of care, a breach of that duty, causation and damage. The standard of care in diagnosis and treatment is judged by the Bolam test as qualified by Bolitho.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>A. TRUE</strong> &mdash; This is the <em>Bolam</em> test: a clinician is judged against a responsible body of practitioners skilled in that field, not against the best practice, and a difference of opinion between experts does not itself establish negligence.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>B. TRUE</strong> &mdash; Under <em>Bolitho</em>, the court may reject a body of opinion that is not logically defensible, for example one that ignores obvious risks and benefits.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>C. FALSE</strong> &mdash; Damage is an essential element; a negligent act that causes no harm gives no cause of action in clinical negligence.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>D. TRUE</strong> &mdash; The claimant must show that, on the balance of probabilities (more than 50%), the breach caused the damage.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>E. FALSE</strong> &mdash; Disclosure of risk is judged by the patient-centred material-risk test in <em>Montgomery</em> (2015), not the Bolam test.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Medical Ethics and Law</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">Ethical analysis in UK medicine commonly uses the four principles of Beauchamp and Childress. Legal duties on confidentiality, candour and the standard of care sit alongside these and are frequently tested in FFPMRCA medicolegal questions.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Four Principles of Biomedical Ethics</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Principle</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Pain medicine example</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Autonomy</strong></td><td>Respect for the capacitous patient&rsquo;s right to make their own decisions.</td><td>Accepting a patient&rsquo;s informed refusal of an injection.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Beneficence</strong></td><td>Act to benefit the patient.</td><td>Offering an effective, evidence-based treatment plan.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-maleficence</strong></td><td>Avoid causing harm.</td><td>Declining to escalate opioids when harms outweigh benefits.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Justice</strong></td><td>Fair distribution of benefits, risks and scarce resources; equal cases treated equally.</td><td>Allocating limited neuromodulation implants by transparent criteria of need and likely benefit.</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">Principles can conflict (e.g. autonomy vs non-maleficence); the clinician&rsquo;s task is to balance them. Double effect, paternalism and fidelity are related concepts.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Confidentiality (GMC, UK GDPR and Data Protection Act 2018)</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Position</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Direct care</strong></td><td>Information can be shared within the team providing direct care on the basis of <strong>implied consent</strong>, provided patients are aware of how their information is used.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Family and friends</strong></td><td>Adults with capacity decide who is told; relatives have no automatic right to information.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Required by law</strong></td><td>Disclosure is required by statute or court order (e.g. notifiable diseases, some regulatory requests).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Public interest</strong></td><td>Disclosure without consent may be justified to prevent or detect serious crime or serious harm; take only the information needed, tell the patient first where safe to do so, and document the reasoning.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Driving</strong></td><td>If a patient continues to drive against advice despite a condition that makes driving unsafe, the GMC advises informing the <strong>DVLA medical adviser</strong>, after warning the patient.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>After death</strong></td><td>The duty of confidentiality continues after a patient&rsquo;s death.</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Duty of Candour</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Type</th><th>Applies to</th><th>Requirement</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Statutory (England)</strong></td><td>The <strong>organisation</strong> (registered provider): Regulation 20, Health and Social Care Act 2008 (Regulated Activities) Regulations 2014</td><td>After a <strong>notifiable safety incident</strong> (unintended or unexpected incident that results in, or could result in, death or severe/moderate harm or prolonged psychological harm), tell the patient as soon as reasonably practicable, give a truthful account, apologise, offer support and follow up in writing.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Professional</strong></td><td><strong>Individual</strong> clinicians (GMC/NMC joint guidance)</td><td>Be open and honest with patients when something goes wrong or may have gone wrong: tell the patient, apologise, offer remedy or support, explain short- and long-term effects, and report the incident.</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">An apology or explanation is not an admission of liability (Compensation Act 2006, s.2). Candour arrangements differ in Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Clinical Negligence (English Law)</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Element / test</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Elements</strong></td><td>Claimant must prove <strong>duty of care</strong>, <strong>breach</strong> of that duty, <strong>causation</strong> and <strong>damage</strong>. Breach without damage is not actionable.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Standard of care &mdash; Bolam</strong></td><td>A clinician is not negligent if they acted in accordance with a practice accepted as proper by a responsible body of medical opinion (<em>Bolam v Friern Hospital Management Committee</em> [1957]). Applied to diagnosis and treatment.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Bolitho</strong></td><td>The supporting body of opinion must be <strong>capable of withstanding logical analysis</strong> (<em>Bolitho v City and Hackney HA</em> [1997]); the court may reject a practice that is not logically defensible.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Disclosure of risk</strong></td><td>Not Bolam: judged by the material-risk test in <em>Montgomery</em> (see Consent in UK Practice).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Causation</strong></td><td>&ldquo;But for&rdquo; the breach, on the <strong>balance of probabilities</strong>, would the harm have been avoided?</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hindsight</strong></td><td>The standard is judged on the knowledge and practice at the time of the event, not with hindsight.</td></tr></table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_mtf_thc_cbd_pharmacology_001', 'MTF', '{"dateAdded":"2026-10-17","rotationIndex":180,"stem":"Regarding the pharmacology of THC and cannabidiol (CBD):","choices":["THC is a partial agonist at CB1 receptors","CBD is responsible for most of the psychotomimetic effects of cannabis","CBD is a high-affinity agonist at the CB1 orthosteric site","THC is metabolised to an active metabolite, 11-hydroxy-THC","CBD inhibits CYP2C19"],"correct":[true,false,false,true,true],"explanation":"<p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">THC and CBD are the two principal phytocannabinoids but have very different pharmacology: THC acts directly at CB1/CB2 receptors, whereas CBD has low affinity for them and a broader set of molecular targets.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>A. TRUE</strong> &mdash; THC is a partial agonist at CB1 and CB2. CB1 activation in the CNS explains its psychoactive, cognitive and analgesic effects.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>B. FALSE</strong> &mdash; The psychotomimetic and intoxicating effects are due mainly to THC. CBD is not intoxicating and may attenuate some THC effects.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>C. FALSE</strong> &mdash; CBD has very low affinity for the orthosteric site; it is a negative allosteric modulator of CB1 and acts at other targets including TRPV1 and 5-HT1A.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>D. TRUE</strong> &mdash; THC is hydroxylated in the liver, mainly by CYP2C9 (and CYP3A4), to 11-hydroxy-THC, which is active and at least as potent as THC. It is then oxidised to inactive carboxy-THC.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>E. TRUE</strong> &mdash; CBD inhibits CYP2C19 (and CYP3A4), which increases plasma levels of the active metabolite of clobazam; this is relevant to prescribing of Epidyolex.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Cannabinoid System</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"nice-guideline-btn\" onclick=\"openGuidelineModal(''ng144'',''NG-144 Cannabis Products'')\">\n  <svg width=\"15\" height=\"15\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><path d=\"M14 2H6a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v16a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h12a2 2 0 0 0 2-2V8z\"/><polyline points=\"14 2 14 8 20 8\"/><line x1=\"9\" y1=\"13\" x2=\"15\" y2=\"13\"/><line x1=\"9\" y1=\"17\" x2=\"15\" y2=\"17\"/></svg>\n  NG-144 Cannabis Products\n</button></div><p style=\"margin:14px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">The endocannabinoid system (ECS) is a homeostatic signalling network involved in nociception, mood, appetite, memory, and immune function. It comprises cannabinoid receptors, endogenous ligands (endocannabinoids), and the enzymes that synthesise and degrade them.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Receptors</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Receptor</th><th>Details</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>CB1</strong></td><td>Densely expressed in the CNS (particularly cortex, hippocampus, cerebellum, basal ganglia), with lower peripheral expression. Mediates the psychoactive effects of THC and modulates synaptic neurotransmitter release (both excitatory and inhibitory) via presynaptic inhibition.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>CB2</strong></td><td>Less ubiquitous; acts by inhibiting proinflammatory responses of immune cells such as macrophages, lymphocytes, and microglia. Largely peripheral/immune in distribution, and upregulated in inflammatory states.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Signalling</strong></td><td>Both are G-protein coupled receptors, signalling via inhibition of adenylyl cyclase and reduced cAMP formation.</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Beyond CB1/CB2 — Emerging Targets</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Target</th><th>Details</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>GPR55</strong></td><td>An orphan receptor and its variants have been identified as new endocannabinoid-responsive receptors, sharing numerous cannabinoid ligands with CB1 and CB2, making it a potential therapeutic target for pain.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>PPARs</strong></td><td>Peroxisome proliferator-activated receptors — nuclear transcription factors activated by cannabinoid-like molecules — have also been implicated in modulating inflammatory and neuropathic pain.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>TRPV1</strong></td><td>Transient receptor potential channels, most notably TRPV1, have a prominent role in pain and interact with the endocannabinoid system (e.g. anandamide is a TRPV1 agonist as well as a CB1/CB2 ligand).</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Ligands</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Class</th><th>Details</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Endocannabinoids</strong></td><td>Anandamide and 2-arachidonoylglycerol (2-AG), synthesised on demand from membrane phospholipids, acting as retrograde messengers at presynaptic terminals.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Phytocannabinoids</strong></td><td>THC (partial CB1/CB2 agonist, psychoactive) and cannabidiol (CBD, non-psychoactive, complex/indirect pharmacology).</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Synthetic cannabinoids</strong></td><td>e.g. nabilone (CB1/CB2 agonist, used for chemotherapy-induced nausea/vomiting and occasionally chronic pain).</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Clinical Relevance in Pain Medicine</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Point</th><th>Details</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Preclinical</strong></td><td>Cannabinoids have shown effective anti-nociception in numerous animal models of chronic inflammatory and neuropathic pain, with evidence supporting anti-nociception from dual CB1/CB2 agonists as well as receptor-specific agonists.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Clinical evidence</strong></td><td>Human trial evidence is more limited and heterogeneous than preclinical data, particularly for chronic non-cancer pain, with modest effect sizes and significant variability between studies.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Direction of travel</strong></td><td>Clinical interest is moving toward peripherally-restricted or receptor-selective agonists to try to separate analgesic benefit from central psychoactive/psychotomimetic side effects.</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Phytocannabinoids &mdash; THC versus CBD</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Feature</th><th>THC (&Delta;9-tetrahydrocannabinol)</th><th>CBD (cannabidiol)</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Receptor action</strong></td><td>Partial agonist at CB1 and CB2</td><td>Very low affinity for the CB1/CB2 orthosteric site; acts as a negative allosteric modulator of CB1, with actions at TRPV1, 5-HT1A and other targets</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Psychoactivity</strong></td><td>Responsible for the &ldquo;high&rdquo;, anxiety and psychotomimetic effects</td><td>Not intoxicating; may attenuate some THC effects</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Metabolism</strong></td><td>Hepatic CYP450 (mainly CYP2C9, also CYP3A4) to <strong>11-hydroxy-THC</strong>, an active metabolite, then to inactive 11-nor-9-carboxy-THC</td><td>Hepatic (CYP2C19, CYP3A4); <strong>inhibits CYP2C19</strong>, raising levels of the active metabolite of clobazam</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Examples</strong></td><td>Dronabinol; component of nabiximols</td><td>Epidyolex; component of nabiximols</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">UK Licensing and Prescribing</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Product</th><th>Composition</th><th>UK status / licensed use</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Nabilone</strong></td><td>Synthetic THC analogue (CB1/CB2 agonist)</td><td>Licensed for chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting unresponsive to conventional antiemetics. Off-label use in pain.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Nabiximols (Sativex)</strong></td><td>Oromucosal spray, THC:CBD approximately 1:1</td><td>Licensed as add-on for moderate to severe spasticity in multiple sclerosis not responding to other antispasticity drugs. Not licensed for pain.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cannabidiol (Epidyolex)</strong></td><td>Purified CBD oral solution</td><td>Licensed with clobazam for seizures in Dravet syndrome and Lennox-Gastaut syndrome (and tuberous sclerosis complex).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dronabinol, herbal/flower, oils</strong></td><td>THC or whole-plant products</td><td>Dronabinol has no UK licence for pain. Others are unlicensed &ldquo;specials&rdquo; (cannabis-based products for medicinal use in humans, CBPMs).</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">Since 1 November 2018, CBPMs have been lawful to prescribe as Schedule 2 controlled drugs (Misuse of Drugs Regulations 2001, as amended). Prescribing may be <strong>initiated only by doctors on the GMC specialist register</strong>, usually after specialist multidisciplinary advice; GPs may continue treatment started by a specialist under shared care.</p><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">Most CBPMs are unlicensed, so prescribing is on a named-patient basis with the prescriber taking responsibility. Cannabis is a Schedule 1 drug under the 2001 Regulations unless it is a CBPM; recreational cannabis remains Class B under the Misuse of Drugs Act 1971.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">NICE NG144 &mdash; Cannabis-based Medicinal Products</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Indication</th><th>NICE recommendation</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting</strong></td><td>Offer nabilone to adults if conventional antiemetics have failed. Do not offer nabiximols, dronabinol or CBD.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>MS spasticity</strong></td><td>Consider nabiximols as add-on if other treatments have not worked; review at 4 weeks and continue only if there is a clinically meaningful (at least 20%) improvement.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Severe treatment-resistant epilepsy</strong></td><td>Consider Epidyolex with clobazam for Dravet syndrome and Lennox-Gastaut syndrome.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Chronic pain in adults</strong></td><td><strong>Do not offer</strong> cannabis-based medicinal products (THC with or without CBD, nabilone, dronabinol) for chronic pain outside a clinical trial, because of insufficient evidence of benefit and concern about harms.</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Adverse Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>System</th><th>Effect</th></tr><tr><td><strong>CNS</strong></td><td>Drowsiness, dizziness, impaired cognition and coordination, anxiety, dysphoria, impaired driving ability</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Psychiatric</strong></td><td>Psychotic symptoms, particularly with high-potency THC and in those predisposed; dependence</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cardiovascular</strong></td><td>Tachycardia, postural hypotension</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Respiratory</strong></td><td>Little or no clinically important respiratory depression (in contrast to opioids)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Withdrawal</strong></td><td>Irritability, insomnia, reduced appetite, restlessness after abrupt cessation following prolonged use</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Other</strong></td><td>Dry mouth, increased appetite, nausea; THC is a specified drug under the 2015 drug-driving regulations</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">Cannabinoids are contraindicated or used with great caution in patients with a personal or family history of psychosis, severe cardiovascular disease, and in pregnancy.</p></div></div>\n</div></div>","relevantReading":[{"label":"NICE NG144: Cannabis-based medicinal products","source":"NICE","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng144"}]}'::jsonb, 'published')
  on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into questions (id, type, data, status) values ('q_sba_nabiximols_ms_spasticity_001', 'SBA', '{"dateAdded":"2026-10-17","rotationIndex":181,"question":"A 38-year-old woman with multiple sclerosis has moderate to severe spasticity that has not responded to baclofen and tizanidine. Which cannabis-based product holds a UK marketing authorisation for this indication?","choices":["Nabilone","Nabiximols","Cannabidiol (Epidyolex)","Dronabinol","Herbal cannabis flower"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">Nabiximols (Sativex) is an oromucosal spray containing approximately equal amounts of THC and CBD. It is licensed in the UK as add-on treatment for moderate to severe MS spasticity that has not responded to other antispasticity drugs, and NICE NG144 supports a 4-week trial with continuation only if there is meaningful benefit.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> Nabilone is a synthetic THC analogue licensed for chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting, not spasticity.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:8px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>B. Correct.</strong> Nabiximols is licensed for MS spasticity.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:8px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>C. Incorrect.</strong> Epidyolex (purified CBD) is licensed with clobazam for seizures in Dravet syndrome and Lennox-Gastaut syndrome, not spasticity.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:8px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>D. Incorrect.</strong> Dronabinol (synthetic THC) has no UK licence for MS spasticity or pain; NICE advises against its use for these indications.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:8px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> Herbal cannabis is an unlicensed product and has no UK marketing authorisation for any condition.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Cannabinoid System</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"nice-guideline-btn\" onclick=\"openGuidelineModal(''ng144'',''NG-144 Cannabis Products'')\">\n  <svg width=\"15\" height=\"15\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><path d=\"M14 2H6a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v16a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h12a2 2 0 0 0 2-2V8z\"/><polyline points=\"14 2 14 8 20 8\"/><line x1=\"9\" y1=\"13\" x2=\"15\" y2=\"13\"/><line x1=\"9\" y1=\"17\" x2=\"15\" y2=\"17\"/></svg>\n  NG-144 Cannabis Products\n</button></div><p style=\"margin:14px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">The endocannabinoid system (ECS) is a homeostatic signalling network involved in nociception, mood, appetite, memory, and immune function. It comprises cannabinoid receptors, endogenous ligands (endocannabinoids), and the enzymes that synthesise and degrade them.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Receptors</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Receptor</th><th>Details</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>CB1</strong></td><td>Densely expressed in the CNS (particularly cortex, hippocampus, cerebellum, basal ganglia), with lower peripheral expression. Mediates the psychoactive effects of THC and modulates synaptic neurotransmitter release (both excitatory and inhibitory) via presynaptic inhibition.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>CB2</strong></td><td>Less ubiquitous; acts by inhibiting proinflammatory responses of immune cells such as macrophages, lymphocytes, and microglia. Largely peripheral/immune in distribution, and upregulated in inflammatory states.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Signalling</strong></td><td>Both are G-protein coupled receptors, signalling via inhibition of adenylyl cyclase and reduced cAMP formation.</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Beyond CB1/CB2 — Emerging Targets</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Target</th><th>Details</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>GPR55</strong></td><td>An orphan receptor and its variants have been identified as new endocannabinoid-responsive receptors, sharing numerous cannabinoid ligands with CB1 and CB2, making it a potential therapeutic target for pain.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>PPARs</strong></td><td>Peroxisome proliferator-activated receptors — nuclear transcription factors activated by cannabinoid-like molecules — have also been implicated in modulating inflammatory and neuropathic pain.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>TRPV1</strong></td><td>Transient receptor potential channels, most notably TRPV1, have a prominent role in pain and interact with the endocannabinoid system (e.g. anandamide is a TRPV1 agonist as well as a CB1/CB2 ligand).</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Ligands</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Class</th><th>Details</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Endocannabinoids</strong></td><td>Anandamide and 2-arachidonoylglycerol (2-AG), synthesised on demand from membrane phospholipids, acting as retrograde messengers at presynaptic terminals.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Phytocannabinoids</strong></td><td>THC (partial CB1/CB2 agonist, psychoactive) and cannabidiol (CBD, non-psychoactive, complex/indirect pharmacology).</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Synthetic cannabinoids</strong></td><td>e.g. nabilone (CB1/CB2 agonist, used for chemotherapy-induced nausea/vomiting and occasionally chronic pain).</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Clinical Relevance in Pain Medicine</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Point</th><th>Details</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Preclinical</strong></td><td>Cannabinoids have shown effective anti-nociception in numerous animal models of chronic inflammatory and neuropathic pain, with evidence supporting anti-nociception from dual CB1/CB2 agonists as well as receptor-specific agonists.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Clinical evidence</strong></td><td>Human trial evidence is more limited and heterogeneous than preclinical data, particularly for chronic non-cancer pain, with modest effect sizes and significant variability between studies.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Direction of travel</strong></td><td>Clinical interest is moving toward peripherally-restricted or receptor-selective agonists to try to separate analgesic benefit from central psychoactive/psychotomimetic side effects.</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Phytocannabinoids &mdash; THC versus CBD</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Feature</th><th>THC (&Delta;9-tetrahydrocannabinol)</th><th>CBD (cannabidiol)</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Receptor action</strong></td><td>Partial agonist at CB1 and CB2</td><td>Very low affinity for the CB1/CB2 orthosteric site; acts as a negative allosteric modulator of CB1, with actions at TRPV1, 5-HT1A and other targets</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Psychoactivity</strong></td><td>Responsible for the &ldquo;high&rdquo;, anxiety and psychotomimetic effects</td><td>Not intoxicating; may attenuate some THC effects</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Metabolism</strong></td><td>Hepatic CYP450 (mainly CYP2C9, also CYP3A4) to <strong>11-hydroxy-THC</strong>, an active metabolite, then to inactive 11-nor-9-carboxy-THC</td><td>Hepatic (CYP2C19, CYP3A4); <strong>inhibits CYP2C19</strong>, raising levels of the active metabolite of clobazam</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Examples</strong></td><td>Dronabinol; component of nabiximols</td><td>Epidyolex; component of nabiximols</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">UK Licensing and Prescribing</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Product</th><th>Composition</th><th>UK status / licensed use</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Nabilone</strong></td><td>Synthetic THC analogue (CB1/CB2 agonist)</td><td>Licensed for chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting unresponsive to conventional antiemetics. Off-label use in pain.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Nabiximols (Sativex)</strong></td><td>Oromucosal spray, THC:CBD approximately 1:1</td><td>Licensed as add-on for moderate to severe spasticity in multiple sclerosis not responding to other antispasticity drugs. Not licensed for pain.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cannabidiol (Epidyolex)</strong></td><td>Purified CBD oral solution</td><td>Licensed with clobazam for seizures in Dravet syndrome and Lennox-Gastaut syndrome (and tuberous sclerosis complex).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dronabinol, herbal/flower, oils</strong></td><td>THC or whole-plant products</td><td>Dronabinol has no UK licence for pain. Others are unlicensed &ldquo;specials&rdquo; (cannabis-based products for medicinal use in humans, CBPMs).</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">Since 1 November 2018, CBPMs have been lawful to prescribe as Schedule 2 controlled drugs (Misuse of Drugs Regulations 2001, as amended). Prescribing may be <strong>initiated only by doctors on the GMC specialist register</strong>, usually after specialist multidisciplinary advice; GPs may continue treatment started by a specialist under shared care.</p><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">Most CBPMs are unlicensed, so prescribing is on a named-patient basis with the prescriber taking responsibility. Cannabis is a Schedule 1 drug under the 2001 Regulations unless it is a CBPM; recreational cannabis remains Class B under the Misuse of Drugs Act 1971.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">NICE NG144 &mdash; Cannabis-based Medicinal Products</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Indication</th><th>NICE recommendation</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting</strong></td><td>Offer nabilone to adults if conventional antiemetics have failed. Do not offer nabiximols, dronabinol or CBD.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>MS spasticity</strong></td><td>Consider nabiximols as add-on if other treatments have not worked; review at 4 weeks and continue only if there is a clinically meaningful (at least 20%) improvement.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Severe treatment-resistant epilepsy</strong></td><td>Consider Epidyolex with clobazam for Dravet syndrome and Lennox-Gastaut syndrome.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Chronic pain in adults</strong></td><td><strong>Do not offer</strong> cannabis-based medicinal products (THC with or without CBD, nabilone, dronabinol) for chronic pain outside a clinical trial, because of insufficient evidence of benefit and concern about harms.</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Adverse Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>System</th><th>Effect</th></tr><tr><td><strong>CNS</strong></td><td>Drowsiness, dizziness, impaired cognition and coordination, anxiety, dysphoria, impaired driving ability</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Psychiatric</strong></td><td>Psychotic symptoms, particularly with high-potency THC and in those predisposed; dependence</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cardiovascular</strong></td><td>Tachycardia, postural hypotension</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Respiratory</strong></td><td>Little or no clinically important respiratory depression (in contrast to opioids)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Withdrawal</strong></td><td>Irritability, insomnia, reduced appetite, restlessness after abrupt cessation following prolonged use</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Other</strong></td><td>Dry mouth, increased appetite, nausea; THC is a specified drug under the 2015 drug-driving regulations</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">Cannabinoids are contraindicated or used with great caution in patients with a personal or family history of psychosis, severe cardiovascular disease, and in pregnancy.</p></div></div>\n</div></div>","relevantReading":[{"label":"NICE NG144: Cannabis-based medicinal products","source":"NICE","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng144"}]}'::jsonb, 'published')
